# Connector scope lock

Canonical: `docs/foundations/CONNECTOR-ENGINE.md` · philosophy: `docs/foundations/CONNECTED-BUSINESS.md`

## Clean rule

**Platform-wide infrastructure powers DigitalGate.**  
**Organisation connectors connect a specific business to its own external systems and data.**

| Scope | Who configures | Examples |
|-------|----------------|----------|
| **Platform** | DigitalGate once | AI Gateway, OpenAI, Anthropic, ElevenLabs, Resend, Twilio (CPaaS), Cloudflare, Stripe (DG billing), Vercel, Sentry, Dreamscape |
| **Organisation** | Each business | Gmail / Microsoft 365, GBP, Meta, LinkedIn, WordPress, Shopify, Xero, Domain, REA, Cotality, OTAs |
| **Business identity** (org) | Each business | Mailbox, SMS sender/number, business phone, WhatsApp Business, social pages, commerce accounts |

Twilio / Resend / ElevenLabs: DigitalGate owns the **provider relationship**; the org may own **numbers, mailboxes, messaging identities, routing**.

## Credential rule

**Never duplicate platform credentials at organisation level** unless the external provider genuinely requires customer-owned credentials, or BYOK is an explicit commercial product.

- Good: DigitalGate → one OpenAI key → Model Router → orgs  
- Bad: every org brings its own OpenAI / ElevenLabs key by default

## Customer UX

- Settings → **Connected Services** = “Connect your business” — by **capability** (Communications, Web, Social, Finance, Industry), not API catalogs.
- Do **not** ask customers to configure platform APIs.
- Operator **Connector Engine** = scopes, probes, credentials, platform-ready health.
- Telephony customer copy: “Connect business phone / SMS” — never “Connect Twilio”.

## Flow

`External system → Connector → DigitalGate Core → Business Brain → Intelligence → Advisor → Action`
